<Window x:Class="RxVerifyOverlay.Integrated.IntegratedBoxesWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Rx Verify — Integrated"
        WindowStyle="None"
        AllowsTransparency="True"
        Background="Transparent"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        Topmost="True"
        ResizeMode="NoResize"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual"
        Focusable="False"
        ShowActivated="False">
    <!--
        INTEGRATED MODE boxes layer (see
        Integrated/IntegratedOverlayCoordinator.cs) — borderless and
        transparent so it visually disappears except for the verdict
        outlines it draws, and CLICK-THROUGH so the pharmacist keeps
        typing/clicking into PioneerRx underneath it with zero difference
        in behavior. AllowsTransparency/Background=Transparent here only
        make the WINDOW itself paintable as see-through; click-through
        itself is a separate Win32 extended style
        (WS_EX_TRANSPARENT | WS_EX_LAYERED | WS_EX_NOACTIVATE) applied in
        code-behind's OnSourceInitialized, since WPF has no first-class
        API for it.

        Positioned every refresh tick to exactly cover PioneerRx's own
        window bounds (IntegratedOverlayCoordinator.UpdateBoxes ->
        RepositionPhysical, in PHYSICAL pixels — see
        NativeWindowPositioning.cs) — this Canvas is the coordinate space
        every box (added in SetBoxes) is positioned within, in DIPs
        relative to this window's own top-left.

        Background DELIBERATELY UNSET (verdict-tooltips-reports branch —
        was "Transparent"): WPF only hit-tests a Panel's own bounds when
        its Background is an actual Brush, even a fully transparent one —
        an unset Background lets a click fall through to whatever's behind
        it instead. This matters now that IntegratedBoxesWindow.xaml.cs
        dynamically clears WS_EX_TRANSPARENT while the cursor hovers a
        verdict bar's hotspot (see that file's class doc, HOVER section):
        with Background="Transparent" here, clearing the Win32 style would
        have made this ENTIRE Canvas (i.e. the whole PioneerRx-sized
        window) swallow clicks, not just the small hotspot Border elements
        SetBoxes/AddHotspot add as children — those children set their own
        Background explicitly (Brushes.Transparent) specifically so THEIR
        bounds stay hit-testable while this root Canvas's empty space
        does not.
    -->
    <Canvas x:Name="BoxCanvas" />
</Window>
